Actually, I want `p/10^k / den = p / (10^k · den)`. If `p` is divisible by `den`, result is `(p/den) / 10^k`. If `p` is not divisible by `den`, we can extend precision by appending zeros to both numerator and denominator... but `10^k` is the decimal place. So to divide `p/10^k` by `den`, the result is `p / (10^k · den)` = `(p·1^i) / (10^k · 10^i · den)` = `(p·10^i) / (10^(k+i) · den)`. We need `p·10^i` divisible by `den`. Then `(p·10^i)/(10^(k+i)·den)` = `(p·10^i) / den / 10^(k+i)`.

So result = `q=bcdiv(p·10^i, den)`, `k_new = k + i`. My code does exactly this: appends i zeros to p (equivalent to multiplying by 10^i), increments k by i, then divides p by den. That's correct!

